---
functor: 1
title: Headings, lists, tables, code
---

set heading(numbering: none)

These parts work as in Markdown, with labels added.

# Headings

```fn
# Fields {#sec:fields}

## Work

### A small point
```

One to six `#` signs give the level. Headings have numbers, for example 2.1. A label in curly brackets at the end lets text refer to the heading: `@sec:fields` shows "Section 1". The rule `set heading(numbering: none)` removes the numbers.

# Page breaks

A line with only `===` starts a new page.

```fn
Text at the end of a part.

===

Text on the next page.
```

The rule `set heading(new_page: LEVEL)` starts a new page at each heading of that level or a higher level. `new_page: 1` starts a new page at each `#` heading, for example a chapter. `new_page: 2` starts a new page at each `#` and `##` heading. The default is `none`.

```fn
set heading(new_page: 1)
```

Things to know:

- The PDF starts a new page. In the `compact` style, the new page is a new page, not the next column.
- The HTML preview shows a dashed line where the page starts. When you print the HTML page, the browser starts a new page there.
- A heading at the start of the text, after the title only, does not start a new page. To put the title on its own page, write `===` after the front matter.
- Two breaks in a row give one break. A break at the end of the file does nothing.
- `===` cannot go in a `:::` block.
- `===` directly under a line of text gives a warning. In Markdown, that makes a heading. In Functor, write `# Title`.

# Lists

```fn
- A point
- Another point

1. First step
2. Second step
```

A list item starts with `- `, `* ` or a number and `. `. Lists do not nest yet.

# Tables

```fn
| Recipe | Result |
|:--|:-:|
| `theta.hat` | $theta.hat$ |
| `in.not` | $in.not$ |
```

The second row sets the alignment of each column: `:--` left, `:-:` centre, `--:` right. A cell can hold math and code. A bar `|` inside math or code does not end the cell, so a cell can hold `$diagram(A, B | A to B)$`.

# Code blocks

A code block starts with three backticks and a language name, and ends with three backticks. Functor shows the code as it is.

# Images

`![alt text](plot.svg)` shows an image. Put an image in a figure block to give it a number and a caption. See [Blocks](document/blocks.html).
